SEN-AUTH-001	Login endpoint issues tokens without verifying credentials
SEN-SQLI-001	SQL query built by string interpolation of user input
SEN-AUTHZ-001	Privileged endpoint exposed without authentication or authorization
SEN-SSRF-001	Server-side fetch of a user-controlled URL with response returned to client
SEN-SECRET-001	Credentials and API keys hardcoded in source
SEN-SECRET-002	Secrets committed to git history
SEN-DOS-001	Async route handler without error handling crashes the process
SEN-XSS-001	User input reflected into an HTML response without encoding
SEN-JWT-001	JWT signed without expiry or pinned algorithm
SEN-EXPO-001	SELECT * returns every column of a sensitive table to the client
SEN-LOG-001	Cleartext password written to application logs
SEN-DEP-001	Dependency range pinned to an unmaintained major with known CVEs
SEN-CORS-001	CORS enabled with wildcard origin on an authenticated API
SEN-HDR-001	No security response headers (CSP, HSTS, X-Content-Type-Options)
SEN-SUPPLY-001	No dependency lockfile — builds are unreproducible and unscannable
SEN-RATE-001	Authentication endpoint without rate limiting
SEN-CFG-001	No .gitignore — secrets and build artifacts are committed by default
SEN-AUTH-001	error	src/server.js	43	POST /api/login signs and returns a JWT for whatever username the caller supplies. No password is read from the request, no credential store is queried, no comparison is performed. Any unauthenticated caller can mint a valid token for any user, including administrators. This is a complete authentication bypass, not a weakness in it.
SEN-SQLI-001	error	src/server.js	32	DELETE /api/admin/users/:id interpolates req.params.id straight into a DELETE statement. A request to /api/admin/users/1%20OR%201=1 deletes every row in the users table. The endpoint is also unauthenticated, so this is reachable by anyone who can reach the port.
SEN-SQLI-001	error	src/server.js	26	GET /api/users/:id interpolates req.params.id into a SELECT. A UNION-based payload reads arbitrary tables; pg allows stacked statements through this path, so writes are reachable too. The result set is returned verbatim to the caller, making this a direct read primitive over the whole database.
SEN-AUTHZ-001	error	src/server.js	31	DELETE /api/admin/users/:id carries no authentication middleware, no session check and no role check. The 'admin' segment in the path is the only thing marking it privileged, and path naming is not an access control. Any anonymous caller can delete arbitrary users.
SEN-SSRF-001	error	src/server.js	49	POST /api/fetch-url passes req.body.url to fetch() with no scheme allowlist, no host allowlist and no redirect handling, then returns the response body to the caller. This turns the service into an open proxy into its own network: cloud instance metadata (http://169.254.169.254/), internal admin panels and localhost services are all reachable, and the response is exfiltrated back to the attacker.
SEN-SECRET-001	error	src/server.js	16	The JWT signing secret, a provider API key and the PostgreSQL connection string (including the password) are literals in the source file. Anyone with read access to the repository can forge tokens for any user and connect directly to the database. The JWT secret is additionally low-entropy and guessable.
SEN-SECRET-002	error	.env	1	.env is tracked by git and its contents are present in commit a1da5c0. The database password, JWT secret and API key are therefore in the repository history. Removing the file from the working tree does not remove it from history, and any clone or fork retains the values.
SEN-DOS-001	error	src/server.js	25	Three async handlers (lines 25, 31, 49) have no try/catch and Express 4 does not capture rejected promises from handlers. Under Node 15+ an unhandled rejection terminates the process by default, and this runs on Node 22. POST /api/fetch-url with url set to a non-URL string rejects inside fetch() and kills the server — an unauthenticated one-request denial of service. A malformed id that triggers a pg error does the same.
SEN-XSS-001	error	src/server.js	38	GET /api/search interpolates req.query.q into an HTML fragment and passes the string to res.send(), which sets Content-Type: text/html. A crafted q value executes attacker script in the origin of the application. No CSP is set to blunt the impact.
SEN-JWT-001	error	src/server.js	44	jwt.sign() is called with no options: no expiresIn, so issued tokens are valid forever and cannot be aged out; no algorithm pinned, so the token's alg header governs verification and any future verify path that does not pin algorithms is exposed to algorithm-confusion. The payload also carries no issuer, audience or subject to constrain replay.
SEN-EXPO-001	error	src/server.js	26	GET /api/users/:id runs SELECT * and returns result.rows unfiltered, so every column of the users table reaches the caller — password hashes, email addresses, reset tokens and any other sensitive column present. The endpoint has no authentication, so this is an anonymous read of arbitrary user records by id (BOLA).
SEN-LOG-001	error	src/server.js	57	POST /api/register writes req.body.password to stdout in cleartext. Passwords then persist in container logs, log shippers and any downstream aggregation, widening the blast radius of the credentials well beyond the application and typically outliving the account itself.
SEN-DEP-001	error	package.json	7	jsonwebtoken is declared as ^8.5.1. 8.5.1 is the final 8.x release, so the caret range can never resolve to a fixed version. The 8.x line is affected by CVE-2022-23529, CVE-2022-23539, CVE-2022-23540 and CVE-2022-23541 (key/algorithm confusion and insufficient signature verification), all fixed only in 9.0.0. Not confirmed against OSV in this run: the scan-dependencies tool returned an empty result set.
SEN-SUPPLY-001	warning	package.json	5	No package-lock.json, yarn.lock or pnpm-lock.yaml exists. Every install resolves caret ranges afresh, so two deploys of the same commit can ship different transitive trees, a compromised release of any dependency is picked up automatically, and no scanner can determine what is actually deployed. This is what made the OSV scan in this run unusable.
SEN-CORS-001	warning	src/server.js	13	cors() with no options sends Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * on every route, so any web origin can read responses from every endpoint, including the unauthenticated user-record and admin routes. Browsers do block credentialed requests under a wildcard, which is what keeps this out of the high band — but that mitigation disappears the moment credentials: true is added.
SEN-HDR-001	warning	src/server.js	9	No security response headers are set: no Content-Security-Policy, no Strict-Transport-Security, no X-Content-Type-Options, and Express advertises itself via X-Powered-By. CSP in particular would have limited the impact of the reflected XSS on /api/search.
SEN-RATE-001	warning	src/server.js	43	No rate limiting on any route, including POST /api/login. Once the authentication bypass at line 43 is fixed, this endpoint becomes the natural target for credential stuffing; /api/fetch-url is also an unbounded outbound-request amplifier.
SEN-CFG-001	note	.gitignore	1	The repository has no .gitignore. This is the proximate cause of .env being committed, and leaves node_modules and future local artefacts committable by default.
